.TH CI_LIBRARY_CLEANUP 3 "19 May 2009"
.SH NAME
ci_library_cleanup \- c-ci library deinitialization
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ci.h>

void ci_library_cleanup(void)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The
.B ci_library_cleanup
function uninitializes the c-ci library, freeing all resources
previously acquired by \fIci_library_init(3)\fP when the library
was initialized, provided there was only one single previous call to
\fIci_library_init(3)\fP. If there was more than one previous call to
\fIci_library_init(3)\fP, this function uninitializes the c-ci
library only if it is the call matching the call to
\fIci_library_init(3)\fP which initialized the library
(usually the very first call to \fIci_library_init(3)\fP).
Other calls to \fIci_library_cleanup(3)\fP have no effect other than
decrementing an internal counter.
.PP
This function must be called when the program using c-ci will
no longer need any c-ci function. Once the program has called
\fIci_library_cleanup(3)\fP sufficiently often such that the
library is uninitialised, it shall not make any further call to any
c-ci function.
.PP
This function does not cancel any pending c-ci lookups or requests
previously done. Program  must use \fIci_cancel(3)\fP for this purpose.
.PP
.B This function is not thread safe.
You have to call it once the program is about to terminate, but this call must
be done once the program has terminated every single thread that it could have
initiated. This is required to avoid potential race conditions in library
deinitialization, and also due to the fact that \fIci_library_cleanup(3)\fP
might call functions from other libraries that are thread unsafe, and could
conflict with any other thread that is already using these other libraries.
.PP
Win32/64 application DLLs shall not call \fIci_library_cleanup(3)\fP from
the DllMain function. Doing so will produce deadlocks and other problems.
.SH AVAILABILITY
This function was first introduced in c-ci version 1.7.0 along with the
definition of preprocessor symbol \fICI_HAVE_CI_LIBRARY_CLEANUP\fP as an
indication of the availability of this function. Reference counting in
\fIci_library_init(3)\fP and \fIci_library_cleanup(3)\fP, which requires
calls to the former function to match calls to the latter, is present since
c-ci version 1.10.0.  Earlier versions would deinitialize the library on the
first call to \fIci_library_cleanup(3)\fP.
.PP
Since the introduction of this function, it is absolutely mandatory to call it
for any Win32/64 program using c-ci.
.PP
Non-Win32/64 systems can still use c-ci version 1.7.0 without calling
\fIci_library_cleanup(3)\fP due to the fact that \fIcurrently\fP it is nearly
a do-nothing function on non-Win32/64 platforms.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ci_library_init (3),
.BR ci_cancel (3)
